# Batch 40 (2026-11-27): deep prehistory; Neolithic/Bronze Age Europe and early writing; world myths; modern anomalies, relics and hoaxes
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None,statusNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  if statusNote:d['statusNote']=statusNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: deep prehistory
E+=[
X('venus-dolni-vestonice','The Venus of Dolní Věstonice: the oldest known fired-clay figure, with a child\'s fingerprint',H,'confirmed',2,-29000,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "A small ceramic statuette of a nude woman, 11.1 cm tall, was found broken in two in a hearth at the mammoth-hunters' camp of Dolní Věstonice in Moravia (Czech Republic) on 13 July 1925. It is dated to about 31,000-27,000 years ago (Gravettian) and, with other figures from nearby sites, is among the oldest known fired ceramic objects in the world, made some 15,000 years or more before pottery vessels appear in the region. It was modelled from local loess mixed with water and fired at a relatively low temperature (500-800 °C). The site also produced clay animals (bears, lions, mammoths, horses, rhinos, owls) and more than 2,000 balls of burnt clay. A tomographic scan in 2004 found the fingerprint of a child, estimated at 7-15 years old, fired into the surface.",
 [('Fertility goddess or something else?','The traditional label "Venus" reflects early 20th-century assumptions that such figures were fertility or mother goddesses. Other proposals include self-representations, magic objects, or teaching or initiation figures; the evidence of the makers\' intent is limited, so none is established.'),
  ('Clay that was meant to explode?','Many of the Dolní Věstonice clay figures are cracked in ways consistent with thermal shock. Some researchers have suggested that wet clay figures were deliberately placed in fire to shatter, perhaps as a ritual or divination; others think the cracking was accidental. This remains a hypothesis.'),
  ('Who made it?','The child\'s fingerprint shows a child handled the figure before firing, but researchers who studied it consider the child an unlikely maker; it shows the object passed through several hands.')],
 ['wiki:Venus_of_Dolní_Věstonice','wiki:Dolní_Věstonice_(archaeological_site)'],img='File:Vestonicka venuse edit.jpg',dateNote='Made c. 31,000-27,000 years ago; found 13 July 1925'),
X('venus-of-willendorf','The Venus of Willendorf and the stone that crossed the Alps',H,'confirmed',2,-28000,['archaeological','petrological'],
 "The Venus of Willendorf is an 11.1 cm figurine of a woman carved from oolitic limestone and tinted with red ochre, found on 7 August 1908 by a workman during excavations by Josef Szombathy, Hugo Obermaier and Josef Bayer near Willendorf in Lower Austria. It is associated with the Gravettian culture and estimated from surrounding layers to have been left in the ground about 30,000 years ago. Her face is not shown; the head is covered by bands that may be plaited hair or a headdress. The stone is not local. A 2022 study using micro-CT scans compared the stone with oolite samples from across Europe and concluded that the closest and most likely source is in northern Italy near Lake Garda, on the far side of the Alps, with a less likely match in eastern Ukraine about 1,600 km away. It is in the Natural History Museum in Vienna.",
 [('Mother goddess','A long-standing interpretation is that "Venus" figurines across Ice Age Europe represented a fertility or mother goddess. Critics note that this idea was shaped by 19th- and 20th-century assumptions and cannot be tested directly.'),
  ('Self-portrait','Catherine McCoid and LeRoy McDermott proposed that such figures were made by women looking down at their own bodies, explaining the proportions and lack of a face; others, such as Michael Bisson, criticised this reading.'),
  ('Long-distance movement','If the Lake Garda source is right, either the stone or the figurine (or its makers) moved hundreds of kilometres across or around the Alps, which supports the view that Gravettian groups were highly mobile and connected across large areas.')],
 ['wiki:Venus_of_Willendorf','doi:10.1038/s41598-022-06799-z'],img='File:Venus von Willendorf 01.jpg',dateNote='c. 30,000 years ago; found 7 August 1908; stone sourced in 2022'),
X('skhul-qafzeh','Skhul and Qafzeh: early modern humans and the oldest burials in the Levant',H,'confirmed',2,-118000,['fossil','archaeological','dating (TL, ESR, U-series)'],
 "Between 1931 and 1935 Theodore McCown excavated the remains of seven adults and three children at Es-Skhul cave on Mount Carmel, part of Dorothy Garrod's excavations; further remains were excavated at Qafzeh near Nazareth from the 1930s and especially the 1960s-70s. First classed in 1939 as 'Palaeoanthropus palestinensis', they are now classified as Homo sapiens, among the earliest of our species in Eurasia, dated to roughly 90,000-120,000 years ago. Several individuals appear to have been deliberately buried, making them among the oldest known burials anywhere. At Qafzeh, marine shells carried from the Mediterranean about 35 km away and lumps of red ochre were found, and perforated Nassarius shells were also recovered at Skhul, suggesting ornaments.",
 [('A failed or early wave out of Africa','Many researchers see Skhul and Qafzeh as an early dispersal of modern humans that did not lead directly to later Eurasian populations; Neanderthals later occupied the region (for example at Kebara and Tabun). Others argue the two groups overlapped or alternated with climatic changes.'),
  ('Mixed features','Some of the skulls show a mix of modern and archaic traits, and some researchers have suggested contact or interbreeding with Neanderthals; genetics now shows interbreeding happened somewhere, but whether these particular people were involved is unknown.'),
  ('Were they really burials?','Most researchers accept deliberate burial for several individuals, such as Qafzeh 11, a child buried with deer antlers; a minority has questioned how intentional some of the burials were.')],
 ['wiki:Skhul_and_Qafzeh_hominins','doi:10.1016/j.jhevol.2005.04.006'],img='File:Skhul 5 cast, norma frontalis.jpg',end=-88000,dateNote='c. 120,000-90,000 years ago; excavated 1931-1935 (Skhul) and 1930s-1970s (Qafzeh)'),
X('misliya-jawbone','The Misliya jawbone: modern humans out of Africa 50,000 years earlier than thought?',H,'disputed',3,-183000,['fossil','dating (U-series, ESR, TL)'],
 "In January 2018 a team led by Israel Hershkovitz published a left upper jaw with teeth (Misliya-1) from Misliya Cave on Mount Carmel, Israel. Using several dating methods on the jaw and associated burnt flints, they dated it to between about 177,000 and 194,000 years ago and attributed it to Homo sapiens, found with Levallois stone tools. At the time this made it the oldest known modern human fossil outside Africa, pushing back the first dispersal by at least 50,000 years compared with Skhul and Qafzeh.",
 [('Modern human','The authors argued that the teeth and jaw anatomy fall within the range of Homo sapiens, consistent with genetic studies that suggest early contacts between modern humans and Neanderthals well before 100,000 years ago.'),
  ('Not enough to be sure','Some palaeoanthropologists noted that a partial jaw alone is limited evidence and that dental features can overlap between hominin groups, so its classification remains debated.'),
  ('Many waves out of Africa','Together with the Apidima skull in Greece, Misliya is often cited for the view that modern humans left Africa repeatedly, in several waves, many of which did not leave descendants among people living today.')],
 ['wiki:Misliya_Cave','doi:10.1126/science.aap8369'],img='File:Misliya cave in Megadim Cliff, Mount Carmel.jpg',dateNote='c. 194,000-177,000 years ago; published January 2018'),
X('apidima-skull','Apidima 1: a 210,000-year-old modern human in Greece?',H,'disputed',3,-208000,['fossil','dating (uranium-thorium)','CT reconstruction'],
 "Two partial skulls were found embedded in breccia in Apidima Cave on the Mani peninsula of southern Greece in 1978. In July 2019 a team led by Katerina Harvati published CT reconstructions and uranium-thorium dates: Apidima 2, with a heavy continuous brow ridge, was identified as Neanderthal-like and dated to more than 170,000 years ago; Apidima 1, the back of a skull, was dated to more than 210,000 years ago and identified as an early Homo sapiens because of its rounded rear profile. If correct, it is the oldest modern human fossil outside Africa and more than 150,000 years older than other Homo sapiens finds in Europe.",
 [('Earliest Homo sapiens in Eurasia','Harvati and colleagues argued that a group of early modern humans reached south-east Europe and was later replaced by Neanderthals, matching the idea of several failed dispersals out of Africa.'),
  ('Not a modern human','Other researchers argued that a partial skull vault without a face is not enough to classify it, questioned whether the two skulls are really of different ages, and in 2020 a team led by Henry de Lumley attributed both skulls to evolved European Homo erectus/early Neanderthal-related populations.'),
  ('Wider implications','Supporters see the find as consistent with genetic evidence for an early exchange of genes from modern humans into Neanderthals; sceptics see the conclusion as resting on a single, incomplete fossil.')],
 ['wiki:Apidima_Cave','doi:10.1038/s41586-019-1376-z'],dateNote='Apidima 1 dated >210,000 years ago; found 1978; published 2019'),
X('cerutti-mastodon','The Cerutti Mastodon: humans in California 130,000 years ago?',H,'disputed',2,-128700,['palaeontological','archaeological (claimed)','dating (uranium-thorium)'],
 "During freeway works on State Route 54 in San Diego County in 1992, palaeontologist Richard Cerutti found mastodon remains, excavated by the San Diego Natural History Museum in 1992-1993. In 2017 a team led by Steven Holen and Thomas Deméré published in Nature that the bones, dated by uranium-thorium to about 130,700 (±9,400) years ago, had been broken while fresh with spiral fractures, and were lying with five cobbles bearing use-wear and impact marks that they interpreted as hammerstones and anvils. They concluded that an unknown kind of human was processing mastodon bones in California. Mainstream dating for people in the Americas is generally around 20,000-26,000 years ago at most (for example the White Sands footprints).",
 [('Earliest Americans','The authors argued that the bone breakage pattern, the placement of the cobbles in otherwise fine-grained sediments and experiments breaking elephant bones all point to human activity, implying that some form of Homo, possibly not Homo sapiens, reached the Americas far earlier than any accepted site.'),
  ('Natural or modern breakage','Many archaeologists responded with scepticism: critics argued that natural processes could break bones and move cobbles, noted the absence of sharp stone flakes and other debris typical of tool use, and asked whether the heavy machinery working at the site could have contributed. As Chris Stringer put it, "extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence".'),
  ('Unknown visitors','Some popular writers connect Cerutti to ideas of lost early migrations, including by Denisovans or other archaic humans crossing Beringia; this is speculative and no human remains were found at the site.')],
 ['wiki:Cerutti_Mastodon_site','doi:10.1038/nature22065'],img='File:Cerutti mastodon tusk - full.jpg',dateNote='Bones dated c. 130,700 years ago; found 1992; published 2017'),
X('cosquer-cave','Cosquer Cave: Ice Age paintings behind an underwater entrance',H,'confirmed',2,-25000,['archaeological','radiocarbon','sea-level evidence'],
 "In 1985 the professional diver Henri Cosquer found a submerged entrance in the Calanque de Morgiou near Marseille. It leads through a 175 m tunnel, starting 37 m below sea level, to dry chambers. In July 1991 Cosquer and fellow divers noticed a hand stencil on the wall, and the cave turned out to be filled with Palaeolithic art; it was made public after three divers from Grenoble became lost in the tunnel and died on 1 September 1991. The art falls into two main phases: about 27,000 years ago (Gravettian), including 65 hand stencils, some with apparently missing fingers, and about 19,000 years ago (Solutrean), with animals including horses, ibex, bison and, unusually, 16 marine animals such as seals, great auks and possible jellyfish. When it was painted, sea level was far lower and the coast lay several kilometres away. Rising seas have since flooded much of the cave. A full-size replica, Cosquer Méditerranée, opened in Marseille in 2022.",
 [('Missing fingers','Hand stencils with apparently missing fingers (also seen at Gargas) have been explained as a sign language or hunting signals made by folding fingers, as ritual or frostbite amputation, or as deliberate artistic convention; it is not known which, if any, is right.'),
  ('Doubts at first','When announced, some suspected a hoax because the art was so well preserved and the entrance so unusual; radiocarbon dating of the paintings settled their age, and today their authenticity is accepted.'),
  ('A drowned landscape','The cave is a direct record of how much of the Ice Age coast, where many people probably lived, is now under the sea; the art itself is threatened as sea level continues to rise. Some alternative writers use such cases to argue that whole coastal civilisations may lie unrecorded under the sea; the cave shows that sites do lie underwater, but contains no evidence of anything beyond Palaeolithic hunter-gatherers.')],
 ['wiki:Cosquer_Cave'],img='File:Panneau des chevaux - Cosquer Méditerranée.jpg',end=-17000,dateNote='Art c. 27,000 and c. 19,000 years ago; entrance found 1985; art found 1991'),
X('teshik-tash','Teshik-Tash: a Neanderthal child ringed by ibex horns?',H,'disputed',3,-68000,['fossil','archaeological','ancient DNA'],
 "In 1938 the Soviet archaeologist Alexey Okladnikov excavated Teshik-Tash cave in the Baysuntau mountains of Uzbekistan and found the skull and bones of a child aged about 8-11, with Mousterian stone tools. He reported that five pairs of Siberian ibex horn cores had been placed around the child's head in a shallow pit, and the find was widely presented as a Neanderthal ritual burial. The skull was reconstructed from about 150 fragments, and the child is classed as a Neanderthal. In 2007 ancient DNA from the bones showed a Neanderthal mitochondrial sequence, extending the known range of Neanderthals about 2,000 km further east into Central Asia (and, with another sample, into Siberia). The age is commonly given as Middle Palaeolithic, perhaps about 70,000 years ago, but is poorly constrained.",
 [('Ritual burial','Several researchers have taken the horn ring as evidence that Neanderthals buried their dead with symbolic acts, alongside other claimed Neanderthal burials such as Shanidar and La Chapelle-aux-Saints.'),
  ('No burial at all','Others point out that the cave contained 761 ibex bones and that the excavation was poorly published. Paul Mellars questioned whether the horns were deliberately placed, and Robert Gargett argued it was not a burial at all.'),
  ('Wider debate','Teshik-Tash is part of the long debate over whether Neanderthals had symbolic behaviour; newer finds such as Shanidar Z (2018) have shifted many researchers towards accepting some deliberate burial, but this case itself remains uncertain.')],
 ['wiki:Teshik-Tash','doi:10.1038/nature06193'],img='File:Skull of Teshik-Tash Boy.jpg',dateNote='Middle Palaeolithic (often given as c. 70,000 years ago); excavated 1938'),
X('anzick-child','Anzick-1: the only Clovis burial, and a genome that answered an old question',H,'confirmed',2,-10950,['archaeological','radiocarbon','ancient DNA'],
 "In 1968 construction workers Ben Hargis and Calvin Sarver found the remains of a child aged 1-2, covered in red ochre, with more than 115 stone and antler tools, on the Anzick family's land near Wilsall, Montana. Dated to about 12,990-12,840 years ago, it is the only known human burial associated with the Clovis culture, whose distinctive spear points were long thought to mark the first Americans. In 2014 the child's genome, the first complete ancient Native American genome, was published. It showed ancestry from Siberia-derived populations and a direct relationship to many present-day Native Americans, especially in Central and South America. After consultation with tribes in Montana, the child was reburied in an intertribal ceremony on 28 June 2014; the artefacts are curated by the Montana Historical Society.",
 [('Answer to the "Solutrean hypothesis"','Dennis Stanford and Bruce Bradley had proposed that Clovis technology came from Ice Age Europeans crossing the Atlantic. The Anzick genome showed no such European ancestry and a close link to Native Americans, and most researchers regard it as strong evidence against that hypothesis; its proponents argue a single individual cannot settle the question.'),
  ('Ethics of ancient DNA','Because the remains were on private land, US federal repatriation law did not apply, and some Native American community members objected that they had not been consulted before the genome was studied. The case led to closer collaboration between geneticists and tribes, and the reburial was carried out jointly.'),
  ('Clovis not first','Clovis is no longer considered the first culture in the Americas; sites such as Monte Verde and the White Sands footprints are older. Anzick-1 nevertheless shows that Clovis people were ancestors of many later Native Americans.')],
 ['wiki:Anzick-1','doi:10.1038/nature13025'],dateNote='c. 12,990-12,840 years ago; found 1968; genome 2014; reburied 28 June 2014'),
]
# --- part 2: Copper and Bronze Age Europe and Anatolia, and disputed early writing
E+=[
X('arslantepe-swords','Arslantepe: a palace before writing, and the world\'s oldest known swords',H,'confirmed',2,-3400,['archaeological','metallurgical','radiocarbon'],
 "Arslantepe is a 30-metre-high mound in the Malatya plain of eastern Turkey, near the upper Euphrates, occupied from at least the 6th millennium BCE to medieval times; it was later the Neo-Hittite city of Melid. Excavated by the Sapienza University of Rome for decades, it is best known for its Late Chalcolithic 'palace complex' of the late 4th millennium BCE, contemporary with the Uruk period of Mesopotamia. Thousands of clay sealings found there show a sophisticated system for controlling the storage and distribution of goods before writing was in use. The complex also produced arsenical-copper weapons, including what UNESCO describes as the earliest swords so far known in the world. The settlement was burned around 3000 BCE, and a later 'Royal Tomb' of the Early Bronze Age contained a richly equipped man accompanied by other individuals. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2021.",
 [('State before writing','Arslantepe is often cited as evidence that centralised political authority and bureaucracy could develop without writing, using sealing systems instead; how far it was an independent development or a response to Uruk Mesopotamia is debated.'),
  ('Swords and elite power','The excavators argue the swords were displayed by an elite as symbols of new political power and mark the beginning of organised combat as an elite privilege; others stress that their exact use, in war or display, cannot be directly observed.'),
  ('Human sacrifice?','Some of the individuals in the Royal Tomb were young people buried around the main burial; the excavators have interpreted them as possible sacrificial victims, but this interpretation is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Arslantepe'],img='File:Hittite lions, Arslantepe 01.jpg',end=-3000,dateNote='Palace complex c. 3400-3000 BCE; site occupied 6th millennium BCE to medieval times'),
X('el-argar','El Argar: a Bronze Age state in Iberia where women were buried with diadems',H,'confirmed',2,-2200,['archaeological','radiocarbon','osteological'],
 "The Argaric culture flourished in south-eastern Iberia (Almería, Murcia and neighbouring areas) from about 2200 to 1550 BCE, and is named after the site of El Argar in Antas, excavated by the Belgian brothers Henri and Louis Siret in the 1880s. Its people lived in fortified hilltop settlements, buried their dead beneath the floors of their houses in cists, pits and large jars (pithoi), and produced large numbers of copper and bronze weapons such as halberds, daggers, swords and axes, and silver ornaments. At La Almoloya (Murcia), excavators found a large hall with benches, interpreted as an assembly or governing space, and Grave 38 (excavated from 2014), a double burial of a man and a woman, in which the woman was buried with a silver diadem and most of the rich goods.",
 [('A ruling class of women?','The La Almoloya excavators (Lull, Micó, Rihuete-Herrada and others) argue that some women held political power, perhaps as rulers. Others accept high status for some women but caution that rich grave goods may reflect family wealth, religious roles or marriage alliances rather than political office.'),
  ('One of Europe\'s first states','Some researchers interpret the Argaric world as an early class-based state with an exploitative elite; others see it as a set of competing chiefdoms. Its sudden end around 1550 BCE, with settlements abandoned, has been linked to social revolt, environmental degradation or climate change.'),
  ('Myth links','Some popular writers have linked the rich Bronze Age cultures of southern Iberia to legends of Tartessos or Atlantis; no archaeological evidence connects El Argar with these later stories.')],
 ['wiki:El_Argar','doi:10.15184/aqy.2021.8'],end=-1550,dateNote='c. 2200-1550 BCE; excavated by the Siret brothers from the 1880s; La Almoloya Grave 38 from 2014'),
X('los-millares','Los Millares: a Copper Age fortress town with 70 tombs',H,'confirmed',3,-3200,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Los Millares, 17 km north of Almería in Spain, was discovered in 1891 during railway construction and first excavated by Luis Siret. It was occupied from about 3200 BCE to about 2200 BCE and may have held around 1,000 people. It is defended by three concentric lines of stone walls, the outer one more than 200 m long with 19 semicircular bastions and a fortified gate, and is surrounded by four smaller outlying forts guarding the approaches. One building inside the walls contained evidence of copper smelting. Its cemetery has about 70 tholos (beehive-vaulted) collective tombs with 'port-hole' entrance slabs. It is the type site of the Chalcolithic Millaran culture.",
 [('Colonists from the east?','Early researchers, including Siret, thought the tholos tombs and metallurgy were introduced by colonists from the eastern Mediterranean, comparing them with Mycenaean tombs. Radiocarbon dating later showed that Los Millares is older than the Mycenaean tholoi, and most archaeologists now see it as a local development.'),
  ('War and inequality','Many archaeologists interpret the fortifications and tombs as evidence of a stratified and often warring society; others think the walls were partly symbolic, built to display a community\'s strength.')],
 ['wiki:Los_Millares'],img='File:Los Millares recreacion cuadro.jpg',end=-2200,changed='1890s: Siret attributes the site to eastern Mediterranean colonists. From the 1970s radiocarbon dates show it predates Mycenaean parallels, and it is reinterpreted as a local development.',dateNote='c. 3200-2200 BCE; discovered 1891'),
X('hochdorf-chieftain','The Hochdorf chieftain: an untouched Celtic prince\'s grave',H,'confirmed',2,-530,['archaeological'],
 "Near Hochdorf an der Enz in Baden-Württemberg, Germany, an amateur archaeologist noticed in 1968 a burial mound that had been worn down by erosion and ploughing to about 1 m high from an original 6 m. Excavated in 1978-1979 under Jörg Biel, it contained an unrobbed burial chamber of about 530 BCE (Hallstatt period). A man about 50 years old and 1.8 m tall lay on a 2.75 m bronze couch on eight wheels, wearing a gold-plated torc, a gold bracelet, a birch-bark hat, a gold-covered dagger and thin gold plaques on his shoes. At his feet was a cauldron with three lions on the rim, made in a Greek workshop in southern Italy, which had held about 400 litres of mead. The tomb also contained an iron-plated four-wheeled wagon with bronze dishes and drinking horns, enough to serve nine people, and traces of hemp.",
 [('Prince, priest or chieftain?','The man is usually interpreted as a ruler of a local Early Celtic elite, whose wealth came from controlling trade with the Greek and Etruscan Mediterranean. Some researchers suggest religious roles, as with other Hallstatt burials.'),
  ('Funeral feast','Analysis of the cauldron and drinking horns has been read as evidence of elaborate feasting and gift-giving, like the feasting described in later Greek accounts of Celts; the number of drinking horns suggests a planned symbolic banquet rather than a single meal.'),
  ('Why it survived','Most such burials were robbed in antiquity. Archaeologists suggest Hochdorf survived because its chamber was buried under a massive stone and earth mound; it is one of the few undisturbed elite graves of its time.')],
 ['wiki:Hochdorf_Chieftain\'s_Grave'],img='File:Hochdorf Chieftain\'s Grave reconstruction.jpg',dateNote='c. 530 BCE; noticed 1968; excavated 1978-1979'),
X('mold-gold-cape','The Mold gold cape and the golden ghost of Goblins\' Hill',H,'confirmed',2,-1750,['archaeological','folklore','documentary'],
 "On 11 October 1833 workmen removing stones from a mound called Bryn yr Ellyllon ('Hill of the Goblins' or 'Hill of the Elves') near Mold in north-east Wales found a skeleton in a stone-lined grave with a crushed cape of sheet gold around its upper body, together with about 200-300 amber beads, of which only one survives. Pieces were shared among the finders and had to be recovered. Reassembled, the cape dates to about 1900-1600 BCE (Early Bronze Age). It was made from a single gold ingot beaten into a sheet and decorated with ribs and bosses; it would have restricted the wearer's arm movement and is thought to be ceremonial. It is in the British Museum. Accounts collected after the find, from the vicar of Mold, the landowner's agent John Langford and others, and published in Archaeologia in 1836, said that before 1833 local people avoided the field because a figure 'clothed in a coat of gold' had been seen there.",
 [('Folk memory','Some writers suggest the ghost story preserved a memory of a gold-clad burial for some 3,500 years, through the name of the mound and tales of a shining figure.'),
  ('After-the-fact embellishment','Others note that all written versions were recorded after the 1833 find, mostly second-hand, so it cannot be proved that the golden details came first; the haunted reputation of a mound called the "Goblins\' Hill" may have been embellished once gold was found.'),
  ('Treasure-guardian tales','Folklorists note that ghosts or spirits guarding barrows and treasure are common across Europe; in this case the tradition and the find happen to coincide, which can be read as either coincidence or genuine memory.')],
 ['wiki:Mold_cape','url:https://www.dailypost.co.uk/news/north-wales-news/guarded-by-a-gold-spectre-2909493|North Wales Daily Post: Guarded by a gold spectre|news','url:https://www.mythslegendsodditiesnorth-east-wales.co.uk/mold-gold-cape|Curious Clwyd: Mold Gold Cape (with transcribed 1830s accounts)|local history'],img='File:The Mold cape.jpg',dateNote='Cape c. 1900-1600 BCE; found 11 October 1833'),
X('hand-of-irulegi','The Hand of Irulegi: the oldest Basque words?',H,'disputed',2,-80,['archaeological','epigraphic'],
 "In June 2021 a team led by Mattin Aiestaran excavating an Iron Age hillfort at Irulegi, near Pamplona in Navarre, Spain, found a flat bronze cut-out of a right hand. On 18 January 2022 the restorer Carmen Usua noticed letters on it, and it was announced on 14 November 2022. It carries five words or groups of letters, first scratched and then punched with dots, in a variant of the north-eastern Iberian script, including a sign known only from the Vascon area. The settlement had been destroyed by fire in the 1st century BCE, during the Sertorian Wars. The first word, sorioneku, has been read by the linguists Joaquín Gorrochategui and Javier Velaza as related to modern Basque zorioneko, '(of) good fortune', and the hand may have hung on a door as a protective amulet.",
 [('First text in the Vasconic language','The research team presented it as the oldest and longest text in the ancient Vasconic language, the ancestor of Basque, showing the Vascones had adapted a script for their own language before the Roman conquest. Velaza points to the special T-shaped sign, otherwise known only from Vascon coins, as evidence of a distinct script adapted for a different language.'),
  ('Sorioneku or sorioneke?','After further cleaning, the specialists studying it now favour the reading sorioneke, which they suggest could be a dedication (dative) to a deity of good fortune, an offering during the Sertorian Wars rather than a door amulet. The Basque philologist Joseba Lakarra argues the word cannot be equated with modern zorioneko, whose meaning he says was only formed in the 18th century.'),
  ('Language not proven','In studies published in January 2024, specialists agreed the piece is authentic and important, but some (for example Julen Manterola and Céline Mounole) said it cannot yet be proven that the language is Vasconic; with a single text and few comparisons, most of the words remain uninterpreted.')],
 ['wiki:Hand_of_Irulegi','url:https://elpais.com/cultura/2024-01-17/los-primeros-estudios-cientificos-creen-que-la-inscripcion-en-la-mano-de-irulegi-es-vasconica-pero-discrepan-sobre-si-es-la-lengua-antecesora-del-euskera.html|El País (17 January 2024): first scientific studies of the Hand of Irulegi (Spanish)|news','url:https://www.eldiario.es/navarra/sorioneku-sorioneke-dudas-mano-irulegi-hallazgo-iba-revolucionar-origen-euskera_1_10824731.html|elDiario.es: From sorioneku to sorioneke - doubts about the Hand of Irulegi (Spanish)|news'],img='File:Irulegiko eskua - Nafarroako Gobernuaren irudi galeria 16.jpg',dateNote='Destroyed with its settlement 1st century BCE; found June 2021; inscription announced 14 November 2022'),
X('must-farm','Must Farm: Britain\'s Bronze Age \'Pompeii\'',H,'confirmed',2,-850,['archaeological','dendrochronology','palaeoparasitology'],
 "Must Farm, in the Flag Fen basin of Cambridgeshire, England, was a small settlement of roundhouses built on stilts above a river in the Late Bronze Age, around the 9th century BCE. Soon after it was built it was destroyed by a catastrophic fire; the platform collapsed into the river, where the silt preserved its contents: whole pots with food residues, fine textiles of plant fibre, wooden tools, a large wooden wheel with its hub, metal tools and weapons, and glass beads. Eight Bronze Age log boats were found nearby in 2011-2012. The Cambridge Archaeological Unit excavated the settlement in 2015-2016. Studies of human and dog coprolites found the earliest evidence in Britain of infection with fish tapeworm and other parasites, consistent with eating raw or undercooked fish and other freshwater animals.",
 [('Attack or accident?','The excavators have discussed whether the fire was an accident or the result of an attack; finds such as unused weapons and the abandonment of possessions leave both possibilities open.'),
  ('Everyday life, not monuments','Supporters of the site\'s importance stress that, unlike Stonehenge or rich burials, it preserves ordinary household life in rare detail, changing the picture of Bronze Age Britain as poor and primitive.')],
 ['wiki:Must_Farm','doi:10.15184/aqy.2019.38','url:https://www.mustfarm.com/|Must Farm excavation project website (Cambridge Archaeological Unit)|project'],img='File:Must Farm CL3 4817 (26447110732) crop.jpg',dateNote='Late Bronze Age, c. 9th century BCE; excavated 2015-2016'),
X('cascajal-block','The Cascajal Block: the first writing in the Americas?',H,'disputed',2,-900,['archaeological','epigraphic'],
 "In the late 1990s road builders quarrying gravel at Lomas de Tacamichapan, Veracruz, Mexico, in the Olmec heartland, found a serpentinite slab about the size of a tablet in a pile of debris. It carries 62 incised signs, some resembling maize, pineapples, insects or fish, arranged in roughly horizontal rows, a system not previously known. Pottery and figurines from the debris led the researchers to date it to the San Lorenzo phase (ending c. 900 BCE). It was published in the journal Science on 15 September 2006 by a team including María del Carmen Rodríguez Martínez, Ponciano Ortíz Ceballos, Michael Coe, Richard Diehl, Stephen Houston, Karl Taube and Alfredo Delgado Calderón. Chemical analysis indicates its stone matches serpentinite from Tehuitzingo, Puebla, a source used for other Olmec objects.",
 [('Earliest New World script','Its publishers argued the block shows a true writing system with sequences of signs, linking the Olmec to literacy centuries earlier than other known Mesoamerican scripts.'),
  ('Writing or symbolism?','Some epigraphers accept that it is authentic but question whether it records language, suggesting a set of symbols or iconographic list instead.'),
  ('Doubts about authenticity','Others, such as Karen Bruhns and Nancy Kelker, questioned its authenticity because it was found out of context in a gravel pile and its row layout is unlike other Mesoamerican writing; David Grove said it "looked like a fake" to him. No second example of the script has been found.')],
 ['wiki:Cascajal_Block','doi:10.1126/science.1131492'],img='File:Cascajal-text.svg',dateNote='Dated by associated finds to c. 1200-900 BCE; found late 1990s; published 15 September 2006'),
X('dispilio-tablet','The Dispilio tablet: Neolithic writing from 5200 BCE?',H,'disputed',3,-5200,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "During George Hourmouziadis's 1993 excavation of a Neolithic lake-shore settlement at Dispilio, on Lake Kastoria in northern Greece, a wooden tablet with incised linear marks was found. It was announced at a symposium in Thessaloniki in February 1994. Its exact archaeological context is not known, because it was found floating in the water that had filled the excavation trench. A single radiocarbon date gives a calibrated age of about 5324-5079 BCE. The wood was damaged after exposure to air and was placed under conservation; as of 2024, no full academic publication of the tablet exists.",
 [('Early writing','Some, including the excavator, have discussed the marks as a possible early sign system and compared them with Neolithic symbols from the Balkans, such as the Vinča symbols and Tărtăria tablets, and with the much later Linear A. Popular websites sometimes present it as proof of a "Neolithic script" thousands of years before Mesopotamia.'),
  ('Not writing','Most specialists say the marks cannot be shown to represent language. Without context or full publication, decoration, ownership marks or counting are all possible, and they warn against strong claims.'),
  ('Hoax rumours','Online claims that the tablet was a hoax or has been deliberately hidden have circulated; there is no evidence of either, and the main reason for the delay in publication is said to be conservation.')],
 ['wiki:Dispilio_Tablet'],dateNote='Radiocarbon-dated c. 5324-5079 BCE; found 1993'),
]
